const description = "The difference between lossy and lossless image compression, which formats use each, and how to choose a quality setting without visible damage.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/lossy-vs-lossless-compression-explained.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Image compression</p>
    <h1>Lossy vs Lossless Compression: What Changes in Your Images</h1>
    <p class="lead">Some ways of making picture files smaller remove detail you may not notice. Others keep every detail but leave the file larger. This guide explains the two approaches in plain words.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Two ways to make a picture smaller</h2>
<p>One way throws away some detail that the eye is unlikely to notice, and the picture becomes much smaller. The other way keeps every detail, so the picture is only a little smaller.</p>
<h2>Which to choose</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>For photos:</strong> the first way works well. A setting of about 75 to 85 usually looks fine.</li>
<li><strong>For screenshots, logos and text:</strong> the second way keeps edges sharp.</li>
</ul>
<h2>A warning</h2>
<p>Each time you save a photo the first way, it loses a little more detail. Keep your original picture and make a smaller copy from it.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>Take a photo of a sunset with smooth gradients. A lossy JPG at quality 80 might be 300 KB and look almost identical to the original. A lossless PNG of the same photo is usually much larger, often several times bigger, because it keeps every pixel.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Which quality setting should I pick for photos?</summary><p>Start between 70 and 85 for photos. Below about 60, artifacts often become visible.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can a WebP file be lossless?</summary><p>Yes. WebP supports both lossy and lossless modes, so it can replace PNG for graphics as well as JPG for photos.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why does saving a JPG repeatedly make it worse?</summary><p>Each lossy save discards more detail. Keep the original and compress from it each time.</p></details>
      </section>
